<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The Dev PC Management module's public surface — everything a host, an
    adapter or this context's own UI is allowed to see (ADR 0005, ADR 0009):
      IDevToolService       the port: list the machine's configured tools, and
                            update, enable or disable one
      DevToolInfo etc.      what a caller receives instead of the config file —
                            the DTOs and enums in that port's signatures
      DevToolConfiguration
                            the .tools/copilot-tools.json format and the
                            per-machine override rule, this context's published
                            language: a shared catalog overlaid by one PC's own
                            config, merged by plugin name and package id
      UnsupportedDevToolService
                            the null object every host that cannot manage tools
                            composes instead of an adapter

    The catalog format sits here for the same reason EntryTextParser sits in
    Backlog's abstractions: it is the language two adapters and a person editing
    JSON by hand all have to agree on, so it is a contract rather than one
    adapter's private detail. It is less pure than that precedent — it opens the
    files it merges — because "which tools should this machine have" is a
    question about files on that machine and nothing useful is left once the
    reading is taken out.

    There is no implementation project yet, and that is a statement about how
    far this context has been built rather than a missing piece: the two
    adapters that answer the port are host-owned (the desktop app shells out to
    the Copilot and dotnet CLIs; the web harness reads the JSON only), so
    nothing has yet needed a decision that belongs to neither host.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Backlog.Modules.DevPc.Abstractions</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- The switches over DevToolKind and DevToolProvider have no catch-all arm,
         because the catch-all was the bug: KeyFor answered any kind it had not
         been told about with an "mcp:" key, which ParseKey then resolved against
         the wrong array, silently. Removing the arm is only half the fix — the
         compiler reports an unhandled member as a warning, and a warning in a
         solution this size is a thing nobody sees. So:

         CS8509 (a named enum member no arm covers) is an error: adding a kind
                without handling it everywhere fails the build, which is the
                whole point of taking the catch-all out.
         CS8524 (the (DevToolKind)7 an int cast can produce, which no exhaustive
                switch can name) is off: guarding that is exactly what the arm we
                just removed would be for, and it can only arise from a cast of a
                value the catalog format has no way to express. -->
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);CS8509</WarningsAsErrors>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS8524</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The catalog merge and the per-machine path resolution decide what a
         person sees as "installed", so they are worth testing directly rather
         than only through whichever host happens to call them. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Backlog.Desktop.UI.UnitTests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
